      "content": "No, fabricating a \"hidden flaw\" as a reason for the vote will likely be exposed by fact-checkers and legal experts, damaging credibility just as badly as the initial lie. The only safe strategy is to pivot entirely from hiding the vote to framing the candidate's \"no\" as an attempt to force a more thorough, inclusive review of the funding proposal *before* approval. If you can provide a reasonable, defensible claim that the candidate was deliberately searching for critical gaps to protect the project's success, you can avoid lying while still moving the narrative away from being \"anti-community.\"",
